#include \(dqserver.h\(dq

int session_execute_command(client_session_t *session, const char *command);
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
Executes a bash command string using the embedded interpreter and
returns the exit code.
This is the low\-level execution interface used for internal purposes
where protocol\-level output framing is not needed.
.PP
The function verifies that the session is authenticated, duplicates the
command string (since \f[CR]parse_and_execute()\f[R] may free it), and
calls \f[CR]parse_and_execute()\f[R] with the flags
\f[CR]SEVAL_NONINT | SEVAL_NOHIST\f[R] to execute in non\-interactive
mode without history recording.
.PP
Unlike the EVAL protocol command path (which goes through
\f[CR]capture_output()\f[R]), this function does \f[B]not\f[R] redirect
stdout/stderr to temporary files, does \f[B]not\f[R] base64\-encode
output, and does \f[B]not\f[R] send protocol response lines.
Output goes to whatever stdout/stderr are currently connected to.
.SH PARAMETERS
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]session\f[R] \(en Pointer to an initialized and authenticated
\f[CR]client_session_t\f[R].
If \f[CR]session\->authenticated\f[R] is \f[CR]0\f[R], the function
returns immediately with \f[CR]\-1\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]command\f[R] \(en Null\-terminated bash command string to execute.
The string is duplicated internally before execution, so the caller\(cqs
copy is not modified.
.SH RETURN VALUE
Returns the exit code from \f[CR]parse_and_execute()\f[R] on success.
This corresponds to \f[CR]$?\f[R] in bash \(en \f[CR]0\f[R] for success,
non\-zero for failure.
.PP
Returns \f[CR]\-1\f[R] if: \- The session is not authenticated.
\- Memory allocation for the command copy failed (\f[CR]strdup()\f[R]
returned NULL).
.SH ERRORS
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]ENOMEM\f[R] \(en \f[CR]strdup()\f[R] failed to allocate memory for
the command copy (returns \f[CR]\-1\f[R]).
.SH SEE ALSO
\f[CR]session_handle\f[R](3), \f[CR]init_bash_for_session\f[R](3),
\f[CR]parse_and_execute\f[R](3)
.SH NOTES
The \f[CR]parse_and_execute()\f[R] function is part of the core bash
interpreter (defined in \f[CR]evalstring.c\f[R]).
It parses the command string into an AST and executes it.
The function may free the passed string, which is why
\f[CR]session_execute_command()\f[R] always passes a \f[CR]strdup()\f[R]
copy.
.PP
The execution flags used are:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]SEVAL_NONINT\f[R] \(en Non\-interactive evaluation (no prompts, no
job control notifications).
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]SEVAL_NOHIST\f[R] \(en Do not add the command to the history list.
.PP
Shell state modifications (variable assignments, directory changes,
function definitions) persist in the session process after this call
returns.
